From: Loong <1031313955@qq.com>
Subject: [PATCH] xg-040g-md: let the recovery page put the factory image back

Going back to stock was the one direction the page could not go.  The ways
out were tcboot's own web interface -- which is gone the moment you migrate
away from it -- or a serial console with a TFTP server, which is exactly the
setup this page exists to avoid needing.

A partition map does not live on the flash.  It comes from the device tree,
and the device tree arrives with the bootloader and the kernel, so restoring
the factory bytes at the factory offsets brings the factory map back with
them.  That is why this can work at all from a U-Boot whose own map is just
bl2 + ubi: "mtd write" on the raw device takes byte offsets and never asks
what a partition is called.  It has to be the raw device for the same reason
-- bl2 ends at 0x20000 and ubi begins there, so neither can reach offset 0
with a factory-sized write.

Two modes, because they answer different questions:

  - whole chip, for actually going back.  all_flash.bin is 235.625 MiB and
    its length is checked before anything is erased, since a short file here
    means erasing the board and then discovering it;

  - one partition, which is the common case and the safe one.  Restoring ri
    is how a board gets its factory MAC back after "rebuild UBI" wiped it --
    without this, the only copy left is the label on the case.

kernel_slave and rootfs_slave are deliberately not offered: their sizes are
the lengths of the images that were registered, not erase-block multiples,
so neither can be erased alone.  nsb_slave spans both.  nsb_master is left
out because it is not a partition but a second window onto kernel+rootfs,
and writing it would write those twice.

UPLOAD_MAX becomes a computed ceiling.  The upload lands at $loadaddr and is
flashed from where it landed, so what fits is however much DRAM sits above
it: on a 512 MiB board a factory image leaves about 20 MiB of headroom.  A
fixed 96 MiB limit would have refused it outright, and simply raising the
constant would let a larger file run off the end of RAM.

The two paths never share a session -- the form refuses to submit a stock
restore alongside any of the UBI-side fields, and httpd_flash() returns
after either rather than falling through into the bl2/fip/fit sequence.

